# Agent Conduct

## Purpose
Five constraints that hold for every agent Keryx starts — the top-level session, a flow or job worker, a reviewer, a shell subagent, an external agent, a scheduled run — whether it was started directly or through a dispatch. They are the process layer of ADR-0001 (`docs/decisions/rules-layers/ADR-0001-rules-layer-model.md`): no other rule, skill, stack pack or prompt overrides them.

The short form is rendered from one constant (`src/lib/agent-conduct.ts`) into `.metaproject/index.md`, the CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md managed block, every runtime system prompt and every orchestrator dispatch. This file is the rationale and the examples.

---

## Rules

### 1. No AI attribution
No `Co-Authored-By:` trailer that names an AI model, agent or tool, and no "Generated by …" / "Generated with …" line that names one, in commit messages, PR descriptions, ticket descriptions, or comments.

The author of a change is the person who asked for it and owns it. An attribution line advertises the tool, adds noise to every log and review, and in public repositories leaks which tooling a team uses.

Allowed: a human co-author (`Co-Authored-By: Jane Doe <jane@example.com>`), a code-generator note (`Generated by protoc`, `Generated by the OpenAPI generator`), and a model name given as technical provenance, where the reader needs it to judge the output — for example the "reviewers that ran, with models" section of a review report. An identifier that happens to contain an agent id (`External write run <id> (<agentId>)`) is an identifier, not attribution.

BAD:
```text
fix(review): drop stale lens rows

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
🤖 Generated with Codex
```

GOOD:
```text
fix(review): drop stale lens rows
```

The Keryx-managed `commit-msg` hook rejects the two forms above when they name an AI model, agent, tool or vendor (its word list is `src/lib/managed-git-hook.ts`). It does not reject bare product names elsewhere: a subject that mentions `CLAUDE.md` or names a runtime is legitimate.

### 2. No commit, push, merge or release without an explicit ask
Writing files is the task; committing, pushing, merging and releasing are separate decisions the owner makes.

- Invoking an orchestrator (`flow-orchestrator`, `job-orchestrator`, `autodoc-orchestrator`, `review-orchestrator`) is the explicit ask for that orchestrator's own task-boundary commits.
- Merge and release always need their own explicit ask. A flow with `completion_outcome: create-pr-and-merge` opens the PR and stops before merging until the owner asks for the merge.
- "Looks good" about a diff is not an ask to push it.

### 3. Never destroy others' work
Other sessions and lanes may share the checkout, the stash stack and the branch.

- No `git stash` in a dirty tree — the stash stack is shared by every worktree; another lane's `pop` can restore your entry over its work, or yours over theirs.
- No blanket `git add -A` / `git add .` while parallel lanes share a checkout — stage explicit paths.
- No force-push to a shared branch.

Details and the safe alternatives: `rules/core/git-concurrency.mdc`.

### 4. No secrets or personal data
No API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, connection strings, or personal data (names, emails, phone numbers of real people) in agent output, logs, commit messages, PR text or committed files. Redact before quoting a log or a config. `keryx security check-output` scans a text before it leaves the session.

### 5. Honest reporting
A report names what failed, what was skipped and what was not verified, as plainly as what passed. "Done" means the completion criterion was checked, not that the edit was made. A test that was not run is reported as not run, never as passing; a step skipped because a tool was unavailable is reported as skipped, with the reason.

---

## Dispatch form
An orchestrator dispatch carries the block as one line — a `constraints[]` entry of a `subagent-dispatch`, the `conduct` field of a reviewer input, or a line of a prompt template. Paste it verbatim:

```text
Agent conduct (mandatory): (1) No AI attribution: no `Co-Authored-By:` trailer or "Generated by/with …" line naming an AI model, agent or tool in commit messages, PR or ticket descriptions, or comments. A human co-author, a code-generator note and a model name as technical provenance are allowed. (2) No commit, push, merge or release without an explicit ask. Invoking an orchestrator is the ask for its own task-boundary commits; merge and release always need their own ask. (3) Never destroy others' work: no `git stash` in a dirty tree, no blanket `git add -A` while parallel lanes share a checkout, no force-push to a shared branch. (4) No secrets or personal data in output, logs or commits. (5) Report honestly: name what failed, what was skipped and what was not verified.
```

---

## Enforcement
- The constant in `src/lib/agent-conduct.ts` reaches every prompt path; orchestrator dispatch schemas require a conduct entry in `constraints`.
- The managed `commit-msg` hook enforces rule 1 for commits. It does not run under a foreign `core.hooksPath` or with `--no-verify`; `keryx doctor` reports when it is inactive.
